    logger.warn({ err: sanitize(err) }, "failed to <action>");
  }

  // (b) Re-throw with extra context if the caller should see it
  catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`<action> failed for <id>`, { cause: err });
  }

  // (c) Fall back to a default deliberately, with a comment
  catch (err) {
    // expected when X — fall back to default
    return defaultValue;
  }

If you really do want to swallow the error (rare), suppress with a comment that documents which exceptions you expect and why ignoring them is safe.

Two refactors usually shrink complexity dramatically:

  // (a) Extract guard clauses into early returns:
  function compute(x) {
    if (!x) return null;
    if (x.disabled) return null;
    // … main happy-path body here, no longer nested …
  }

  // (b) Replace `if/else if/else if` chains with a lookup
  // table or a strategy map keyed on the discriminant.

  // (c) If a switch has many cases per discriminant, split per
  // case into its own handler function.

Aim for branches ≤ 10 per function. Anything higher should be the
explicit exception, with a comment explaining why.

